gineering Problem Statement**\n\nA hydraulic pump station transfers oil from an atmospheric reservoir to an elevated hydraulic system. The pump processes a volumetric flow rate of $Q = 0.05\\text{ m}^3/\\text{s}$ for oil with a density of $\\rho = 850\\text{ kg/m}^3$.\n\nGiven the following system conditions:\n\n1. **Fluid Flow & Hydraulic Head (`fluid_flow` & `pump_power`):**\n* Total dynamic head of the pump ($H$) = $45\\text{ m}$.\n* Suction pipe diameter at the inlet ($d_1$) = $0.15\\text{ m}$.\n* Discharge pipe diameter at the outlet ($d_2$) = $0.10\\text{ m}$.\n\n\n2. **Thermal Effects (`thermodynamics`):**\n* Due to fluid friction and internal thermodynamic gains inside the pump housing, the oil temperature increases by $\\Delta T = 1.8\\text{ K}$ between the inlet and outlet.\n* Specific heat capacity of the oil ($c_p$) = $1900\\text{ J/(kg}\\cdot\\text{K)}$.\n\n\n3. **Mechanical Power & Efficiency (`mechanics`):**\n* Overall efficiency of the pump unit ($\\eta_{\\text{overall}}$) = $78\\% = 0.78$.\n\n\n\n---\n\n### **🎯 Required Calculations:**\n\n1. **Inlet and Outlet Fluid Velocities ($v_1, v_2$):**\n* Calculate the fluid velocity in the suction pipe ($v_1$) and discharge pipe ($v_2$) in $\\text{m/s}$.\n\n\n2. **Hydraulic Power ($P_{\\text{hydraulic}}$):**\n* Calculate the static hydraulic power output required to lift the fluid by head $H$ in $\\text{kW}$.\n\n\n3. **Thermal Energy Transfer Rate ($P_{\\text{thermal}}$):**\n* Calculate the rate of thermal energy gained by the fluid due to the temperature rise $\\Delta T$ in $\\text{kW}$.\n\n\n4. **Total Required Pump Shaft Power ($P_{\\text{shaft}}$):**\n* Calculate the total input shaft power required by the pump drive ($\\text{kW}$) by combining the hydraulic, kinetic, and thermal power components using the unified equation:\n\n\n\n$$P_{\\text{shaft}} = \\frac{\\rho \\cdot Q \\cdot \\left( g \\cdot H + \\frac{v_2^2 - v_1^2}{2} + c_p \\cdot \\Delta T \\right)}{\\eta_{\\text{overall}}}$$'}, {'role': 'assist